# W3 T23 (B6) — copy to apps/web/.env.local (the BUILD ROOT) for a configured build.
# `pnpm start` runs the Next app from apps/web, so Next reads apps/web/.env.local — NOT a
# .env.local at the game root. NEVER commit real values: .env.local is gitignored (`.env*` in
# .gitignore); this template carries NAMES only.
#
# The TEST commands (pnpm -r test) do NOT read a .env.local file — they read the SHELL env, so
# export the two variables in your shell before running the suites (e.g. RLS suite below).
#
# Both variables are needed together. With either one unset the app builds as a GUEST-ONLY
# build: no Supabase client is created, no cloud call can be made, and connect-src in the
# Content-Security-Policy stays `'self'` (apps/web/next.config.ts).
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL — the project URL, e.g. https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
#   Only its ORIGIN reaches the CSP (connect-src gets the exact origin, never a wildcard).
#   A malformed non-empty value fails the build on purpose.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY — the publishable key class (sb_publishable_...), the
#   same credential family as A14's GitHub secret. This is a public, browser-shipped
#   credential that is safe ONLY because RLS + the explicit table grants in
#   web_app/supabase/migrations/20261012_auth_profiles_runs.sql are the real control.
#   NEVER put a secret key or the legacy service_role JWT here.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# RLS suite gate (apps/web/src/auth/rls.test.ts). Unset → the suite SKIPS and prints the cases
# that did not run. Set to 1 with the two variables above (and a test project where email
# confirmation is DISABLED, so signup returns a token) to execute the grant/policy probes:
#   SUPABASE_TEST=1 pnpm --filter @airhythm/web test -- rls
SUPABASE_TEST=
